{
  "id": "find-bottom-left-tree-value",
  "questionId": "find-bottom-left-tree-value",
  "slug": "find-bottom-left-tree-value",
  "title": "Find Bottom Left Tree Value",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Tree bfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree bfs",
    "queue"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "the answer is always the first value processed in whichever level turns out to be the last one - can you just track that as you go?",
    "run a normal level order BFS; at the start of processing each level, remember the value of the first node in that level",
    "since you don't know in advance which level is the deepest, just keep overwriting your 'answer so far' with the first node's value of each new level",
    "when the queue finally empties, whatever you last recorded is the answer - no need to detect 'deepest' explicitly"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "recording the LAST node's value of each level instead of the first, which finds the bottom-right value instead",
    "trying to precompute the tree's depth first and then search for it, which is more complex and error-prone than just tracking the last-seen first-of-level value",
    "in a DFS approach, visiting right child before left child, which finds the wrong leftmost node when two branches reach the same max depth"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A binary tree is given as a plain integer array named `tree`, using a\ncomplete-binary-tree (heap) index layout:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`\n- `-101` means \"no node here\" - real node values always fall between -100 and 100, so -101 can never be a genuine value, and an index at or past the end of the array also counts as missing\n\nFor example, `[1, 2, 3, -101, 4]` encodes this tree:\n\n```\n      1\n     / \\\n    2   3\n     \\\n      4\n```\n\nThe deepest level is level 2, whose only real node is 4, so 4 is the answer.\n\n**Find the tree's deepest level, the level furthest from the root that still has at least one real node, and return the leftmost value at that level.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= length of tree <= 2000",
      "every real node value is in the range -100 to 100",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value",
      "the tree always has at least one real node"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-node tree",
      "a deepest level with only one real node",
      "a tree that is a right-leaning chain, where the deepest node is still 'leftmost' because it's the only node on its level"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [3, 9, 20, -101, -101, 15, 7]",
        "output": "15",
        "expectedOutput": "15",
        "explanation": "the deepest level is level 2, containing 15 and 7; the leftmost of those is 15"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]",
        "output": "4",
        "expectedOutput": "4",
        "explanation": "the deepest level is level 2, containing 4, 5, 6, 7 left to right; the leftmost is 4"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1]",
        "output": "1",
        "expectedOutput": "1",
        "explanation": "the only level is the deepest one, and its only value is the leftmost by default"
      }
    ],
